# Target C-II — degree-pure support-six dual blocks cannot have convex reciprocal `H_7`

**Date:** 2026-09-18  
**Status:** proof-only structural strengthening; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** the degree-pure support-six endpoint left after exclusion of the minimal five-face one-anchor block  
**Depends on:** one-anchor transpose-kernel theorem; bounded contact-pentagon no-parallel-side theorem; exact stopped-train occurrence signs; reciprocal unit-force construction used for the support-five block; reciprocal force turn-gap `<2pi/3`; closed-equilateral-pentagon two-turn inequality; source-gap reciprocal hull-angle theorem  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_support6_reciprocal_h7_exclusion_2026_09_18.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The support-five one-anchor relation component is now excluded. The next degree-pure
endpoint has six one-anchor degree-five faces. Its relation graph is forced to be
`K_6` minus a perfect matching. Adding the common zero dual value of the outer/activated
side gives a reciprocal graph

\[
H=K_7\setminus M,
\]

where `M` is a perfect matching on the six non-ground reciprocal vertices.

There is one new degeneracy that did not occur in the five-face case: all six non-ground
dual vectors can in principle coincide. Six parallel anchor vectors can cancel in a
`3+3` orientation split, so parity no longer kills this collapsed branch. This note does
not discard it.

On the complementary, noncollapsed branch the reciprocal unit-force construction is
nondegenerate on every edge of `H`. The inherited pentagon turn-gap forces a strong
local sign rule at every non-ground reciprocal hull vertex. In the fully convex
seven-vertex hull case that rule has only four compatible perfect matchings. Three are
excluded directly by the ground equilibrium. The fourth is excluded by an exact signed
length identity which is a positive sum of strict triangle and convex-quadrilateral
inequalities.

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\text{a noncollapsed degree-pure support-six block cannot have all seven reciprocal points on the convex hull.}
}
\]

Any such noncollapsed block is forced to a five- or six-vertex reciprocal hull, with
the ground reciprocal point on the hull and with one or two non-ground reciprocal
points interior. The all-equal collapsed branch remains separate.

---

## 1. The degree-pure support-six relation graph is `K_6` minus a perfect matching

Let

\[
F_1,\ldots,F_6
\]

be a nonzero one-anchor relation component of support six, and assume the degree-pure
endpoint: each `F_i` is a bounded strict unit-contact pentagon. Each face has exactly
one outside/activated anchor train, hence exactly four residual internal train
occurrences.

The one-anchor theorem proves that every face in a nonzero relation component has
relation degree at least four, and the bounded endpoint transport graph is simple.
Consequently every `F_i` has relation degree exactly four. Thus the relation graph `R`
is a simple four-regular graph on six vertices. Its complement inside `K_6` is
one-regular, so

\[
\boxed{R=K_6\setminus M}
\tag{1.1}
\]

for a perfect matching `M` on `{1,...,6}`.

Let

\[
y_i=c_i a_i\ne0
\qquad (1\le i\le6)
\tag{1.2}
\]

be the six dual face vectors of a nonzero fiber mode, and put

\[
y_0:=0.
\tag{1.3}
\]

The reciprocal adjacency graph obtained by adding the six anchor incidences `0i` is
therefore

\[
\boxed{H=K_7\setminus M,}
\tag{1.4}
\]

where the three missing edges are entirely among the non-ground vertices.

---

## 2. Dual-value dichotomy: either all six non-ground points coincide or all are distinct

The support-five reciprocal argument used parity to exclude coincident non-ground dual
vectors. That argument cannot simply be copied here.

### Lemma 2.1

If `y_i=y_j` for any distinct `i,j in {1,...,6}`, then

\[
\boxed{y_1=\cdots=y_6.}
\tag{2.1}
\]

### Proof

Let the common value be `v`. In `K_6\setminus M`, the vertices `i,j` have at least two
common relation neighbors. Fix one such common neighbor `k`. If `y_k ne v`, then both
nonzero reciprocal differences

\[
y_k-y_i,
\qquad
y_k-y_j
\]

are equal, hence projectively parallel. The transpose equations on the relation edges
`ki` and `kj` make the two corresponding train directions at physical pentagon `F_k`
projectively parallel. The bounded-pentagon no-parallel-side theorem forbids this.
Therefore every common relation neighbor of `i,j` also has dual value `v`.

This already gives at least four non-ground vertices with dual value `v`. Any remaining
vertex has relation degree four, hence is adjacent to at least two vertices in that
four-vertex set. If its dual value differed from `v`, those two incident relation edges
would again produce two projectively parallel sides at its pentagon. Therefore it also
has value `v`. Repeating for the final vertex proves (2.1). `square`

Hence there are exactly two branches:

1. **collapsed branch:** `y_1=...=y_6=v ne0`;
2. **noncollapsed branch:** the six non-ground reciprocal points are pairwise distinct.

The collapsed branch is not contradictory by the parity argument used at support five.
Indeed all six outside anchor directions are then projectively parallel to `v`. Summing
all six physical pentagon closure equations cancels every internal train occurrence and
leaves the six oriented unit anchor sides. Their sum can vanish with a `3+3` orientation
split. No existence claim is made here, but this branch must be retained.

The rest of this note assumes the noncollapsed branch.

---

## 3. Noncollapsed support six gives an exact unit-force reciprocal on `K_7\setminus M`

For each relation edge `ij in E(R)`, the transpose equation says `y_i-y_j` is
projectively parallel to the corresponding complete train direction. Since the
noncollapsed branch makes `y_i-y_j ne0`, there is a unique nonzero signed scalar
`lambda_ij` such that

\[
 y_i-y_j=\lambda_{ij}w_{ij}^{(i)},
\tag{3.1}
\]

where `w_ij^(i)` is the oriented unit side vector contributed by that train to the
closure of `F_i`. At the other endpoint,

\[
w_{ij}^{(j)}=-w_{ij}^{(i)}.
\]

For the outside anchor of `F_i`, the same construction with `y_0=0` gives

\[
y_i-y_0=\lambda_{i0}w_{i0}^{(i)},
\qquad \lambda_{i0}\ne0.
\tag{3.2}
\]

Every physical side vector has unit length, hence for every edge `ij in E(H)`,

\[
\boxed{|\lambda_{ij}|=|y_i-y_j|.}
\tag{3.3}
\]

The five physical side vectors of `F_i` close. Therefore for every non-ground reciprocal
vertex,

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{j:ij\in E(H)}{y_i-y_j\over\lambda_{ij}}=0,
\qquad i=1,\ldots,6.
}
\tag{3.4}
\]

Each summand has unit norm. Summing (3.4) over `i=1,...,6` cancels every internal
relation edge and yields the missing equilibrium at the ground point:

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{j=1}^6{y_0-y_j\over\lambda_{0j}}=0.
}
\tag{3.5}
\]

Thus `H=K_7\setminus M` carries a signed unit-magnitude central-force equilibrium.
Writing

\[
s_{ij}:=\operatorname{sgn}(\lambda_{ij}),
\qquad r_{ij}:=|y_i-y_j|,
\]

and multiplying the vertex equilibria by the reciprocal positions gives the same exact
tensor cancellation as in the support-five reciprocal construction, now over `E(H)`:

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{ij\in E(H)}
 s_{ij}r_{ij}e_{ij}e_{ij}^{T}=0,
}
\tag{3.6}
\]

where `e_ij=(y_i-y_j)/r_ij`. Taking traces gives the signed length balance

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{ij\in E(H)}s_{ij}r_{ij}=0.
}
\tag{3.7}
\]

The source geometry also survives. At each non-ground reciprocal vertex the five force
vectors in (3.4), in cyclic angular order, are exactly the five oriented unit side
vectors of the physical pentagon `F_i`. Therefore every consecutive force-direction gap
is strictly below `2pi/3`.

A small general-position fact will be useful below.

### Lemma 3.1

No three non-ground reciprocal points are collinear.

### Proof

Among any three vertices of `K_6\setminus M`, at most one pair is a missing matching
edge. Hence one of the three vertices is relation-adjacent to the other two. If the
three reciprocal points were collinear, the two nonzero reciprocal differences at that
vertex would be projectively parallel. The corresponding two physical pentagon sides
would be projectively parallel, impossible. `square`

A collinear triple `y_0,y_i,y_j` is not excluded by this argument when `ij` is one of
the missing matching edges. This matters in the nonconvex branches and is another
reason not to import the support-five reciprocal conclusions wholesale.

---

## 4. Every non-ground reciprocal hull vertex has a `2+3` sign split

Fix a non-ground reciprocal point `y_i` which lies on the convex hull of the seven
reciprocal points. The five raw directions along the incident edges of `H` lie in the
open hull cone at `y_i`, of width `phi_i<pi`. The five force vectors are obtained by
possibly reversing individual raw directions.

A `0+5` sign split is impossible because five unit raw directions lying in one open
halfplane cannot sum to zero.

The support-five proof allowed a `1+4` split until a later hull-angle estimate. Here the
closed-pentagon two-turn inequality kills it immediately.

Assume one sign class has one force ray `P` and the other has four consecutive force
rays `N_1,N_2,N_3,N_4`. The raw cone and its antipodal copy are disjoint, so the two sign
classes are contiguous in force-angle order. Let the five consecutive force gaps be
`delta_1,...,delta_5`, with `delta_1` and `delta_5` the two gaps bordering `P`.

For every closed equilateral force pentagon with all individual gaps below `2pi/3`, the
proved two-turn lemma gives

\[
\delta_j+\delta_{j+1}<\pi
\tag{4.1}
\]

cyclically. In particular

\[
\delta_5+\delta_1<\pi.
\tag{4.2}
\]

The angular span of the four-ray `N` block is therefore

\[
\delta_2+\delta_3+\delta_4
=2\pi-(\delta_5+\delta_1)
>\pi.
\tag{4.3}
\]

Reversing all four `N` rays by `pi` to recover their raw directions preserves that span.
But all five raw directions lie in a cone of width strictly less than `pi`. Contradiction.

Hence every non-ground reciprocal hull vertex has an exact

\[
\boxed{2+3\text{ incident sign split}.}
\tag{4.4}
\]

The local alternation proof from the support-five exclusion also uses only the five
incident raw rays, the contiguous `2+3` force blocks, and the two-turn inequality. It
therefore applies verbatim here:

\[
\boxed{
\text{the size-two sign class occupies raw positions two and four.}
}
\tag{4.5}
\]

Equivalently the size-three sign class occupies raw positions one, three, and five.
This is invariant under reversing the raw-ray order.

Finally, the source-gap hull-angle argument also depends only on these same local five
rays and their pentagon force equilibrium. Its proof therefore transfers unchanged and
gives

\[
\boxed{\phi_i>{2\pi\over3}}
\tag{4.6}
\]

at every non-ground reciprocal hull vertex.

---

## 5. Only hull sizes five, six, or seven remain; the nonconvex branches force ground `3+3`

Let the reciprocal convex hull have `h` vertices.

If the ground point were interior, every hull vertex would be non-ground. By (4.6), the
hull angle sum would be strictly larger than

\[
h{2\pi\over3}.
\]

For `h<=6` this is at least the exact polygon angle sum `(h-2)pi`, with strictness at
the endpoint `h=6`; for `h<6` it is larger still. Hence the ground point cannot be
interior. A seven-vertex hull, of course, already contains the ground point.

If the ground point is on a hull with `h<=4`, then the other `h-1` hull vertices are
non-ground and contribute more than `(h-1)2pi/3`, which already exceeds `(h-2)pi` for
`h<=4`. Therefore

\[
\boxed{h\in\{5,6,7\}.}
\tag{5.1}
\]

If `h=5`, the four non-ground hull angles exceed `8pi/3`, so the ground hull angle
satisfies

\[
\phi_0<3\pi-{8\pi\over3}={\pi\over3}.
\tag{5.2}
\]

If `h=6`, the five non-ground hull angles exceed `10pi/3`, so

\[
\phi_0<4\pi-{10\pi\over3}={2\pi\over3}.
\tag{5.3}
\]

At the ground vertex all six reciprocal edges are present. Their raw unit directions
lie in the ground hull cone and satisfy a signed unit-vector equilibrium.

A `0+6` sign split is impossible because the six raw directions lie in a halfplane. A
`1+5` split would require the resultant of five same-cone unit vectors to have magnitude
at most one. Projection onto the cone bisector gives magnitude at least
`5 cos(phi_0/2)`. Thus a `1+5` split requires

\[
\phi_0\ge2\arccos(1/5)>2\pi/3.
\]

A `2+4` split would require the four-vector same-cone resultant, whose bisector
projection is at least `4 cos(phi_0/2)`, to have magnitude at most two. This forces

\[
\phi_0\ge2\pi/3.
\]

Both conditions contradict (5.2) or (5.3). Therefore both nonconvex branches satisfy

\[
\boxed{
H5\text{ or }H6\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\text{the ground reciprocal equilibrium has a }3+3\text{ sign split}.
}
\tag{5.4}
\]

The rest of the note excludes `H7`.

---

## 6. Convex `H7`: exact finite sign compatibility leaves four matchings

Assume all seven reciprocal points are hull vertices. Label them in cyclic hull order

\[
0,1,2,3,4,5,6,
\tag{6.1}
\]

with `0` the ground point. The missing perfect matching `M` is on `{1,...,6}`.

At a non-ground vertex `i`, the raw rays to the other six hull vertices occur in cyclic
order

\[
i+1,i+2,\ldots,i+6\pmod7
\]

or the reverse. Delete the unique matching mate of `i`; the remaining five are exactly
the reciprocal edges incident to `i`. By (4.5), for either fixed global sign color:

- if that color has local degree two, its neighbors are raw positions `2,4`;
- if it has local degree three, its neighbors are raw positions `1,3,5`.

Undirected-edge consistency is now a finite problem. Enumerating the fifteen perfect
matchings on `{1,...,6}` and the `2^6` local choices of degree two or three leaves
exactly four matching types in the fixed cyclic labeling:

\[
\begin{aligned}
M_A&=\{12,36,45\},\\
M_{A'}&=\{14,23,56\},\\
M_B&=\{14,25,36\},\\
M_C&=\{16,25,34\}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{6.2}
\]

For each matching there are exactly two sign assignments, related by global sign
reversal. The exact exhaustive regression is in
`verify_target_c_support6_reciprocal_h7_exclusion_2026_09_18.py`; no floating-point
geometry enters that check.

Choose one representative sign color. Its ground positive-neighbor sets may be taken as

\[
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
M&\text{positive ground edges}&\text{ground sign word on }1,\ldots,6\\
\hline
M_A&\{02,03,05\}&-++-+-\\
M_{A'}&\{02,04,05\}&-+-++-\\
M_B&\{02,05\}&-+--+-\\
M_C&\{02,04,06\}&-+-+-+
\end{array}
\tag{6.3}
\]

The global complement gives the second assignment in each row.

---

## 7. Ground equilibrium excludes `M_A`, `M_{A'}`, and `M_C`

At the ground point write the six raw unit directions in increasing lifted angle order
as

\[
u_j=e^{i\theta_j},
\qquad
\theta_1<\theta_2<\cdots<\theta_6,
\qquad
\theta_6-\theta_1<\pi.
\tag{7.1}
\]

Define the three forward chord vectors on the unit circle

\[
\Delta_{12}=u_2-u_1,
\quad
\Delta_{34}=u_4-u_3,
\quad
\Delta_{56}=u_6-u_5.
\tag{7.2}
\]

Each is nonzero, and

\[
\Delta_{ab}
=2\sin{\theta_b-\theta_a\over2}
 \exp i\left({\theta_a+\theta_b\over2}+{\pi\over2}\right).
\tag{7.3}
\]

Hence

\[
\arg\Delta_{12}
<\arg\Delta_{34}
<\arg\Delta_{56},
\tag{7.4}
\]

and all three arguments lie in an open interval of width less than `pi`.

For `M_A`, the ground sign word `-++-+-` turns the ground equilibrium into

\[
\boxed{\Delta_{12}=\Delta_{34}+\Delta_{56}.}
\tag{7.5}
\]

The right-hand side has argument strictly between `arg Delta_34` and
`arg Delta_56`, hence strictly larger than `arg Delta_12`. Contradiction.

For `M_{A'}`, the word `-+-++-` gives

\[
\boxed{\Delta_{56}=\Delta_{12}+\Delta_{34}.}
\tag{7.6}
\]

The right-hand side has argument strictly between the first two chord arguments, hence
strictly smaller than `arg Delta_56`. Contradiction.

For `M_C`, the alternating word `-+-+-+` gives

\[
\boxed{\Delta_{12}+\Delta_{34}+\Delta_{56}=0.}
\tag{7.7}
\]

But the three nonzero chord vectors lie in one open halfplane, so their sum cannot be
zero. Contradiction.

Global sign reversal changes none of these equations. Thus only `M_B` remains.

---

## 8. The opposite matching `M_B` violates the exact signed length balance

For

\[
M_B=\{14,25,36\},
\tag{8.1}
\]

choose the representative positive edge set

\[
P=\{02,05,13,16,24,35,46\}.
\tag{8.2}
\]

Every other edge of `H=K_7\setminus M_B` is negative. Put

\[
L:=\sum_{e\in P}d_e
 -\sum_{e\in E(H)\setminus P}d_e,
\qquad d_{ij}:=|y_i-y_j|.
\tag{8.3}
\]

The reciprocal trace identity (3.7) requires

\[
L=0.
\tag{8.4}
\]

We prove instead that `L<0` for every strictly convex heptagon in cyclic order
`0,1,...,6`.

Consider the eight strict triangle residuals

\[
\begin{aligned}
T_1&=d_{02}-d_{01}-d_{12},\\
T_2&=d_{16}-d_{01}-d_{06},\\
T_3&=d_{36}-d_{03}-d_{06},\\
T_4&=d_{13}-d_{12}-d_{23},\\
T_5&=d_{24}-d_{23}-d_{34},\\
T_6&=d_{25}-d_{26}-d_{56},\\
T_7&=d_{35}-d_{34}-d_{45},\\
T_8&=d_{46}-d_{45}-d_{56}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{8.5}
\]

All satisfy `T_j<0` because the relevant triples are distinct noncollinear hull points.

For four points `a<b<c<d` on a strictly convex polygon, the crossing diagonals satisfy

\[
d_{ac}+d_{bd}>d_{ab}+d_{cd},
\qquad
d_{ac}+d_{bd}>d_{ad}+d_{bc}.
\tag{8.6}
\]

This follows immediately by splitting both crossing diagonals at their interior
intersection and applying the strict triangle inequality on each side. Apply (8.6) to
obtain four more strict negative residuals:

\[
\begin{aligned}
Q_1&=d_{05}+d_{13}-d_{03}-d_{15},\\
Q_2&=d_{05}+d_{24}-d_{04}-d_{25},\\
Q_3&=d_{02}+d_{46}-d_{04}-d_{26},\\
Q_4&=d_{16}+d_{35}-d_{15}-d_{36}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{8.7}
\]

A direct coefficient collection gives the exact identity

\[
\boxed{
2L=\sum_{j=1}^{8}T_j+\sum_{j=1}^{4}Q_j.
}
\tag{8.8}
\]

Every term on the right is strictly negative, so

\[
\boxed{L<0,}
\tag{8.9}
\]

contradicting (8.4).

The distances `d_25` and `d_36` appearing in the auxiliary residuals are distances
between reciprocal points across missing matching pairs. They are not reciprocal graph
edges and carry no stress. This is harmless: their coefficients cancel identically in
(8.8). The verification script checks the coefficient identity symbolically.

The global-complement sign assignment replaces `L` by `-L`, while the signed length
balance still demands zero, so it is excluded as well.

Therefore `M_B` is impossible.

---

## 9. Consequence and remaining support-six obstruction

Sections 6--8 exclude every sign-compatible perfect matching in the convex seven-point
branch. Therefore a noncollapsed degree-pure support-six one-anchor component satisfies

\[
\boxed{
\text{reciprocal hull size }h\in\{5,6\},
}
\tag{9.1}
\]

with the ground reciprocal point on the hull. The residual alternatives are:

- `H6`: ground on hull, exactly one non-ground reciprocal point interior, ground sign
  split `3+3`;
- `H5`: ground on hull, exactly two non-ground reciprocal points interior, ground sign
  split `3+3`.

Separately, the collapsed branch

\[
y_1=\cdots=y_6\ne0
\tag{9.2}
\]

remains live and corresponds to six projectively parallel outside anchors with oriented
anchor sides summing in a `3+3` split.

This note does **not** exclude all support-six one-anchor dual components and therefore
does not improve the global `n_1/6` corank coefficient by itself. It reduces the sharp
support-six endpoint to two explicit nonconvex reciprocal order-type families plus the
collapsed parallel-anchor branch.

The next finite target is now smaller than an arbitrary `K_7\setminus M` tensegrity:
classify and exclude the `H5/H6` sign/visibility patterns under the five source pentagon
turn-gap constraints, and separately exclude the collapsed branch directly in the
physical train geometry. Excluding **both** residual mechanisms would raise the minimum
support of a nonzero one-anchor dual component from six to seven and improve the
closure-corank coefficient from `n_1/6` to `n_1/7`.

No theorem-state file is changed.

**Disposition:** forced the degree-pure support-six relation graph to `K_6` minus a
perfect matching; isolated the new all-equal dual degeneration; constructed the
noncollapsed unit-force reciprocal `K_7\setminus M`; strengthened every non-ground hull
vertex to the exact `2+3` alternation rule and hull angle `>120` degrees; reduced the
reciprocal hull to `H5/H6/H7`; exhaustively classified the convex `H7` sign-compatible
matchings; and excluded all four by exact ground-equilibrium or convex-distance
arguments. C-II remains open.